A||||6||fierobob||headlight motors and tach||||6||
Z||000000||fierobob||05-30-2000||01:59 PM||||have a question about 88 headlight motors,<BR>the white plastic bushing that turns the acuator arm is slipping is there a repair for this? also other side headlight works fine when car is cold when car is hot it doesn't<BR>work??? where are the relays located?<P>also a question on tachs. mine reads about<BR>1500 rpm too high, is this the filter or the tach?||||reg||6
Z||000002||william_thorniii||05-30-2000||02:05 PM||||bob,<P>The headlight bushings are available from Rodney Dickman.  Hold on to your wallet though because they are a whopping $9 for both sides (including shipping)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  He is a great guy to do business with and often ships the product before receiving payment.  In the mean time, if your only alternative is driving w/o lights... place a quart of oil under the lamp housing to prop it open.  I have been known to drive around for a while like this while waiting for a fix.  <P>The rpm could be tach filter, but honestly I have never had to deal with that... <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||MikeE||05-31-2000||09:18 PM||||I can't vouche for this because I haven't tried it yet but I will have to since I am having a similar prob.  This may not be your problem either.  A friend who has a Fiero took the headlight's hydraulic mechanism apart (you have to drill the rivets and find replacement bolts) and found that his slipping was caused by broken threads on the nylon gear.  He also noticed that the gear only moved through a 180 degree arc so he pulled the gear and rotated it to use the teeth on the unused half.  He says it worked fine.  In addition, he told me the motors are the same as those in the firebird which are easier to locate but again, I can't confirm this.||||reg||1
